course: books
lesson: 33
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter III, part 1: The Wild Wood (winter, and the Mole sets out alone)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Mole has long wanted to meet the famous Badger, whom everyone talks about and nobody sees. But the Rat always puts him off: Badger will turn up some day; he hates invitations; he'd be offended by a visit; and anyway he lives in the very middle of the Wild Wood.
explain: Summer ends. Frost and mud keep them indoors, the river races past too fast for boating, and the Rat sleeps a lot and writes poems by the fire. The animals tell stories of the summer — which the writer paints as a play where the flowers come on stage one by one, until June arrives.
explain: One afternoon, while the Rat dozes in his armchair hunting for rhymes, the Mole quietly decides to go out by himself, explore the Wild Wood, and perhaps meet Mr. Badger.
explain: It is cold and still under a hard steely sky. The bare winter countryside seems to show him «the insides of things», and he likes it plain and strong. Ahead lies the Wild Wood, «low and threatening, like a black reef».
explain: At first it's fun: twigs crackle, logs trip him, fungus on stumps looks like funny faces. But he goes deeper, where the light is dimmer, the trees crowd closer, and holes make «ugly mouths» at him from both sides.
fact: The Badger seemed «such an important personage» who, «though rarely visible», made «his unseen influence felt by everybody about the place» (a personage = an important person).
fact: The Rat: «Badger'll turn up some day or other—he's always turning up ... But you must not only take him as you find him, but when you find him.»
fact: The Mole: «Couldn't you ask him here dinner or something?» The Rat: «He wouldn't come ... Badger hates Society, and invitations, and dinner».
fact: The Rat has «never even ventured to call on him at his own home myself», and says «we can't ... he lives in the very middle of the Wild Wood».
fact: The Mole: «You told me the Wild Wood was all right, you know.» The Rat, «evasively» (avoiding a straight answer): «O, I know, I know, so it is ... But I think we won't go there just now.»
fact: In winter «cold and frost and miry ways kept them much indoors» (miry = muddy), and the swollen river raced past «with a speed that mocked at boating of any sort».
fact: «In the winter time the Rat slept a great deal, retiring early and rising late», and «scribbled poetry or did other small domestic jobs».
fact: Summer is told as a play with flowers as actors: «Purple loosestrife arrived early», then «Willow-herb, tender and wistful, like a pink sunset cloud», then comfrey, then «the diffident and delaying dog-rose», and last «meadow-sweet ... in amber jerkin» — and then «the play was ready to begin» (diffident = shy; a jerkin = an old short jacket).
fact: The Mole set out while the Rat «in his arm-chair before the blaze was alternately dozing and trying over rhymes that wouldn't fit».
fact: «It was a cold still afternoon with a hard steely sky overhead»; Nature «was deep in her annual slumber and seemed to have kicked the clothes off».
fact: «He had got down to the bare bones of it, and they were fine and strong and simple.» — he liked the countryside «undecorated, hard, and stripped of its finery».
fact: «the Wild Wood, which lay before him low and threatening, like a black reef in some still southern sea» (a reef = a line of rocks just under the sea that wrecks ships).
fact: «Twigs crackled under his feet, logs tripped him, funguses on stumps resembled caricatures» — a caricature is a funny drawing that exaggerates a face.
fact: «trees crouched nearer and nearer, and holes made ugly mouths at him on either side.»
fact: Old words: «put off» = told to wait; «ventured» = dared; «out of the question» = impossible; «with much persistence» = again and again; «resolution» = firm decision; «exhilarating» = thrilling.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «ginnel» (North England) = a narrow alley; «dreich» (Scotland) = grey and wet; «cwtch» (Wales) = a cuddle, or a cosy little place; «adulting» = doing grown-up jobs; «it's giving horror film» = it feels like a horror film; «just now» (South Africa) = in a while.
exercise: Who has the Mole long wanted to meet? | The Badger | Toad's housekeeper | The Otter's son | The weasels
exercise: «rarely visible». What does this mean about Badger? | He is hardly ever seen | He is invisible, like a ghost | He is very small | He is blind
exercise: What does the Rat keep saying about Badger? | He'll turn up some day | He's gone away to the Wide World for good | He's dead | He's at Toad Hall
exercise: «take him as you find him, but when you find him». What does this mean? | Accept Badger as he is, and meet him only when he chooses | Look everywhere for Badger, and catch him when you can | Give him a present | Never meet him
exercise: Why won't the Rat invite Badger to dinner? | He hates Society and invitations | He's not a friend of the Rat any more | He eats too much | He's too busy
exercise: Has the Rat ever visited Badger's home? | No, never | Yes, every Sunday for tea | Once, last summer, with the Otter | Yes, by boat
exercise: Where does Badger live? | In the very middle of the Wild Wood | In a hole by the river, next door to the Rat | In Toad Hall | In Town
exercise: «replied the Rat evasively». What does «evasively» mean? | Avoiding a straight answer | Very loudly and clearly | Happily | In a whisper
exercise: What keeps the animals indoors in winter? | Cold, frost and muddy ways | The weasels, who come out of the Wild Wood in winter | Toad and his noisy motor-car | Floods over all the meadows
exercise: Why is there no boating in winter? | The swollen river races too fast | The boats are all frozen into the ice for months | The Rat sold his boat | The Mole forgot how
exercise: What does the Rat do a lot in winter? | Sleeps | Swims with the ducks | Goes to Town | Drives a car
exercise: In the writer's «play» of summer, which flower arrives early? | Purple loosestrife | Meadow-sweet in his amber jerkin | The shy dog-rose | Snowdrops
exercise: Which flower is «diffident and delaying» (shy and late)? | The dog-rose | Purple loosestrife, which came first of all | Willow-herb | Comfrey
exercise: Which flower comes last, so «the play was ready to begin»? | Meadow-sweet | Willow-herb | Comfrey | Purple loosestrife
exercise: What is the Rat doing when the Mole slips out? | Dozing and trying rhymes | Writing a long letter to Toad about his motor-car | Cooking a big hot supper | Sleeping upstairs in his bed
exercise: What does the Mole decide to do? | Explore the Wild Wood and maybe meet Badger | Visit Toad and see his new car | Go back to his old home | Go boating
exercise: What is the weather when he sets out? | Cold and still, with a steely sky | Warm and sunny, with a soft breeze from the south and birds singing | Raining | Snowing hard
exercise: «Nature ... seemed to have kicked the clothes off». What does this mean? | Trees and hedges are bare | It is such a warm day that everyone has taken off their coats | The animals have no clothes | It's windy
exercise: How does the Mole feel about the bare winter country? | He likes it plain and strong | He hates it and wants summer back | He's scared of the bare fields | He's bored of walking alone
exercise: What is the Wild Wood compared to? | A black reef in a still sea | A soft green carpet spread over the hills | A great grey castle on a hill | A soft white cloud in the sky
exercise: What do the funguses on stumps look like? | Caricatures — funny faces | Little cakes that he wants to eat | Lamps glowing in tiny windows | Hats hung up on a row of pegs
exercise: What do the holes do as he goes deeper? | Make ugly mouths at him | Sing to him | Close up behind him | Shine
test: Mason, it's giving horror film (it feels like one): «the Rat said loads of times, "sure, let's go and see Badger tomorrow".» Put it right. | He always put the Mole off | Right: he promised to take him the very next day | He said Badger was dead | He went alone
test: The Rat says the Wild Wood «is» all right, and then «we won't go there just now». What does this tell the Mole, if he'd listened? | The Rat really thinks it's dangerous | The Rat is just lazy and doesn't fancy the long walk in the cold | The wood is lovely | Badger is out
test: Why does the Mole go alone, without telling the Rat? | He's restless and the Rat is dozing | The Rat told him to go and see Badger on his own | He's angry with the Rat | He's lost
test: The writer tells the summer like a play with flowers as actors. Why might he do that? | To show how the summer unfolded, beautiful and in order | Because flowers really do talk in this book, like the animals | To make it scary | To list plant names
test: Looking back: in chapter I, which animals did the Rat say «you can't really trust» in the Wild Wood? | Weasels, stoats and foxes | Squirrels and rabbits, who are a very mixed lot | Otters from the river bank | Moorhens and other water-birds
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did Badger do when he saw the picnic party? | Said «H'm! Company» and left | Sat down and ate with them, then rowed home with the Otter | Shouted | Bit the Rat
test: Looking back: in chapter I, which dark place did the Rat answer «shortly» about, as if he didn't like to talk of it? | The Wild Wood | The river | The backwater by the old mill | His own cosy parlour
test: Looking back: what happened to the Mole the last time he did something without the Rat's say-so, in chapter I? | He grabbed the oars and tipped the boat | He jumped off a bridge into the deep river to swim | He lost the basket | Nothing bad
test: Looking back: at the end of chapter II, what did Toad order? | A large and very expensive motor-car | A new caravan, painted red | A boat | A horse
test: Olly, adulting hard (doing grown-up jobs): «in winter the Rat is up early working all day.» Put it right. | He retires early and rises late | Right: dawn till dusk, every day | He leaves for Town | He goes boating
test: «He had got down to the bare bones of it, and they were fine and strong and simple.» What is the Mole enjoying? | The plain shape of the land | The bones of animals lying about in the frosty fields and ditches | A meal | The cold
test: The Rat kept saying Badger would «turn up». Had he turned up by the winter? | No — so the Mole set off to find him | Yes: he came to tea at the Rat's every week all summer | Yes, once | He sent a letter
test: The Wild Wood is «like a black reef in some still southern sea». Why is a reef a scary thing to compare it to? | Reefs wreck ships that come too close | Reefs are full of colourful fish | Reefs are warm | Reefs are far away
test: «funguses on stumps resembled caricatures». Is the Mole scared of them at first? | No — «that was all fun» | Yes: he runs straight home in terror and hides under his bed | He eats them | He doesn't see them
test: ★★★ Count the flowers named as actors in the summer play: loosestrife, willow-herb, comfrey, dog-rose, meadow-sweet. Which arrives fourth? | The dog-rose | Comfrey | Meadow-sweet | Willow-herb
test: ★★★ «trees crouched nearer and nearer, and holes made ugly mouths». Trees can't crouch. Why does the writer say it? | To show the wood feels alive and threatening to the Mole | Because the trees really move | Because it's a dream | Because the Mole is upside down
test: ★★★ The Mole chose a time when the Rat was dozing. In Alice (lessons 1–24) Alice also slipped into adventure while someone near her was busy. Who? | Her sister, reading a book | The White Rabbit, checking his watch | The Duchess | The Queen
exam: Identify the reason advanced by the Rat for declining to invite the Badger to dine. | That the Badger hated Society, invitations and dinner | That the Badger was abroad | That the Rat disliked him | That the Badger was unwell
exam: Ascertain the place of residence of the Badger as stated by the Rat. | The very middle of the Wild Wood | A hole adjacent to the Rat's own | Toad Hall | The Wide World beyond
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the meteorological conditions upon the Mole's departure. | A cold still afternoon beneath a hard steely sky | A warm afternoon of gentle rain | A blizzard | A gale
exam: It is given that the Mole entered the Wild Wood. Identify the comparison applied by the author to the wood as seen from without. | A black reef in some still southern sea | A green and pleasant garden | A great castle | A sleeping giant
